nodecode-jev-judge is a rewrite of DoGMaTiiC's DoGMaTiiC/hermes-jev:

  https://github.com/DoGMaTiiC/hermes-jev
  commit 5dddbeaac5be08281e6c3b372eeaba3639971f0a
  MIT License, Copyright (c) DoGMaTiiC (plugin.yaml)

Rewritten: the pre-tool gate (__init__.py, gate.py, jev.py) as one :tool hook
on Nodecode's eval: the same three typed questions in the same wire vocabulary
(its boolean is the wire's noul, its impact a score), the same thresholds in
code, the same two modes (shadow logs, enforce asks the operator with Allow
once / Deny), the same fail-open contract on every path with one JSONL line per
decision and its reason, the redaction of key-shaped runs and the 12-argument,
600-character and 65536-character caps, the per-process judgment cache, and the
jev_ask verb, here (jev-judge:ask state questions) in the cell's own package.
TypeSafe's /v1/systemone and OpenRouter's alpha /decisions are carried with
their question shapes and the original's Retry-After handling; the Python
(urllib, stdlib only) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http for the one POST,
nlk:define-section for the settings, and nle:define-cell for what a start
installs and a stop takes back.

Left out: the Vercel AI Gateway backend (AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY), since two
backends cover the keys people hold and a third request and answer shape is a
third thing to keep true; the rate-limit breaker and the min_interval pacing
between outgoing calls (one retry after a short Retry-After remains, and the
cache carries the rest); the opt-in gate_version v2 six-signal ladder, which
would double the paid request for a call the frozen triple already judges; and
Hermes's tool names (terminal, write_file, patch), because Nodecode has one
tool: the section's tools list defaults to eval.
